# The Dockerfile uses an explicit COPY allow-list, so nothing here is load-bearing
# for what ends up in the image. This file exists for the other half of the
# problem: everything below is otherwise uploaded to the daemon as build context
# on every build (the host virtualenv alone is ~750 MB), and it keeps a future
# `COPY . .` from silently re-introducing a secret.

# secrets -- must never reach a layer, a build cache, or a registry
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# runtime data: encrypted blobs, the session vault, the audit chain.
#
# `data/` here is NOT the same rule as `data/` in `.gitignore`, and the
# difference is load-bearing rather than trivia. Docker anchors every pattern to
# the context root, so this line removes `./data` and nothing else. Git's
# grammar makes the identical line match a directory called `data` at ANY depth
# -- including `secure_context_pipeline/detection/data/`, where the gazetteers
# `entities.yaml` requires live, and `load_registry` refuses to start without
# them. Do not "harmonise" the two files by writing `**/data/` here: that ships
# an image whose container boot-fails on registry validation. Pinned by
# `test_every_runtime_asset_the_registry_needs_actually_reaches_the_image`, whose
# expectations were measured against a real build rather than reasoned about.
data/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# host virtualenv -- wrong platform, and ~750 MB of build context
.venv/
venv/

# vcs + agent/editor state
.git/
.gitignore
.claude/
.DS_Store

# python build artefacts and caches
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# demo artefacts
wire_dump*.txt
